board_atmega4809 — modern-AVR (megaAVR 0-series) blink demo
===========================================================

The first simavr example board that targets a *modern* AVR (AVRxt). Every other
board in examples/ drives a classic AVRe/AVRe+ part; this one exercises the
modern engine and peripheral models that distinguish this fork:

  * avr_port_modern  — PA0 driven via the PORT register block (DIRSET/OUTTGL)
  * avr_usart_modern — USART0 greeting + counter, routed to PA4 via PORTMUX
  * avr_clkctrl / sim_interrupts CPUINT path — exercised implicitly at boot

The firmware (atmega4809_blink.c) is written against the device header's
memory-mapped peripheral structs (PORTA, USART0, PORTMUX), not the flat
<avr/io.h> register macros used by the classic examples.

Build & run (from this folder)
------------------------------

Open a console, change into this folder, build, then run:

    cd examples/board_atmega4809     # the demo loads its .axf from here
    make                             # builds the host program + firmware (.axf)
    ./obj-*/blink.elf                # run the demo

The runnable program is the host binary built under obj-<arch>/ (for example
obj-x86_64-pc-linux-gnu/blink.elf); the ./obj-*/ glob just picks it regardless
of architecture. Run it from this folder so it finds atmega4809_blink.axf.

Expected output: the firmware's USART0 greeting and "tick N" counter echoed to
the terminal, interleaved with the first few PA0 toggles, then a summary —
PA0 toggled 20 times over 200 simulated ms — plus a GTKWave-openable trace
written to atmega4809_blink.vcd.

The host program (blink.c) hooks PA0 through the *classic* IOPORT IRQ mesh and
USART0 through the *classic* UART IRQ mesh — the modern PORT and USART models
reuse avr_ioport.h's / avr_uart.h's IRQ/ioctl abstractions, so existing simavr
parts (LEDs, buttons, UART endpoints) and VCD wiring connect to them unchanged.
That is why ./blink can print the serial output directly.

Toolchain note
--------------

Building the firmware needs a modern avr-gcc (12+) that ships the megaAVR-0
device headers (this tree was verified with avr-gcc 16) — the same requirement
as running any firmware on this fork. The Makefile detects whether avr-gcc can
target atmega4809 and, if not, skips the firmware step (so a plain `make` across
examples/ still succeeds) while still building the host binary. Install a modern
avr-gcc and re-run `make` to build atmega4809_blink.axf and run the demo.

Running the firmware under run_avr
----------------------------------

./blink is the full demo (serial echo + PA0 summary + VCD). You can also run the
firmware directly under the run_avr CLI to emit a VCD, e.g. tracing PA0:

    ../../simavr/run_avr -m atmega4809 -f 8000000 \
        --add-trace 'PA0=portpin@0x0400/0x01' \
        -o blink.vcd atmega4809_blink.axf

Note: run_avr only loads firmware and records VCD traces — it does NOT echo
USART to the console (use ./blink for that). It also has no cycle bound, so this
blink (an infinite loop) runs until you stop it with Ctrl-C; the VCD is written
on exit.
